  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Cool start to week, then shower and thunderstorm\nchances return late this week and into the weekend.\n\nBreezy and dry conditions through this evening as temperatures top \nout in the upper 70s to around 80F, with only 60s in the mountains \nfor highs today. Winds quickly diminish this evening. High pressure \nbuilding over the region tonight promotes light to calm winds and \nclear skies. A great setup for radiative cooling that will drop \ntemps to the 50s east of the Blue Ridge, and 40s to the west. The \nhighest locations in the Alleghenies could drop to the upper 30s. \n\nLight east to southeast winds Tuesday and Wednesday as the high \npressure shifts offshore. Expect a gradual warm up with Wednesday's \nforecast highs back into the mid/upper 80s, with mainly 70s for the \nmountains. Despite higher humidity, the pattern does remain dry.\n\nUpper level ridging shifts eastward offshore throughout the day on \nThursday as a cold front drops southeast from the Great Lakes. \nPrimarily dry conditions are expected on Thursday with only a slight \nchances of showers and thunderstorms west of I-81 in the afternoon. \nHigh temperatures will be in the upper 70s to 80s across the \nforecast area with RH values expected to increase through the \nafternoon. \n\nThe approaching cold front brings a good bit of uncertainty with the \nforecast for Friday through the weekend. The main impact will be on \ntemperature forecast with the high temperature spread ranging from \nthe upper 60s to upper 90s depending on the placement of the front \nthis weekend. The first possible solution will be the cooler of the \ntwo with the front passing through the forecast area before stalling \nnear the VA/NC border. The second solution involves the front \nstalling overhead with warmer temperatures expected. \n\nEither way, expect the return of daily shower and thunderstorm \nchances later this week and into the weekend as the front approaches \nand impacts the forecast area. The best chance for storms will be \nFriday as the initial front moves into the Mid-Atlantic and then \nagain on Sunday as an additional front approaches from the \nnorthwest.",
